102
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:29,520
piece of writing in AI. And it says, this is my 
wording by the way. Say past 70 years of entire  

103
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:36,160
AI research can be summarized into developing 
progressively more general method with weaker  

104
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:41,160
modeling assumptions or inductive biases, and add 
more data and compute, in other words scale up. 

105
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:47,280
And that has been the recipe of entire AI 
research, not fancy things. And if you think  

106
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:54,840
about this, the models of 2000 is a lot more 
difficult than what we use now. And so it's much  

107
00:10:54,840 --> 00:11:02,560
easier to get into AI nowadays from technical 
perspective. So this is, I think, the really  

108
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:08,600
the key information. We have this compute cost is 
going down exponentially, and it's getting cheaper  

109
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:13,240
faster than we are becoming a better researcher.
So don't compete with that and just try to  

110
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:19,880
leverage that as much as possible, and that is 
the driving force that I wanted to identify.  

111
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:24,120
And I'm not saying this is the only driving 
force, but this is the dominant driving force,  

112
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:29,080
so we can probably neglect the other ones. So 
here's a graphical version of that. x-axis we have  

113
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:34,200
a compute, y-axis we have a performance of some 
kind. Let's think about some general intelligence. 

114
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:39,560
And let's look at two different methods. One, 
with more structure, more modeling assumptions,  

115
00:11:39,560 --> 00:11:44,960
fancier math, whatever. And then the other one 
is a less structure. What you see is typically,  

116
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:50,320
you start with a better performance when you have 
a low compute regime, and then but it plateaus  

117
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:54,480
because of some kind of structure backfiring.
And then with the less structure because we give  

118
00:11:54,480 --> 00:11:59,080
a lot more freedom to the model, it doesn't work 
in the beginning. But then as we add more compute,  

119
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:06,440
it starts working, and then it gets better, we 
call this more scalable method. So does that mean  

120
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:11,960
we should just go with the least structure, 
most freedom to the model possible way from  

121
00:12:11,960 --> 00:12:16,720
the get go? And the answer is, obviously, no.
Let's think about even less structured case. This  

122
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:24,960
red one here is, it will pick up a lot later and 
requires a lot more compute. So it really depends  

123
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:30,560
on where we are. We cannot indefinitely wait 
for the most general case. And so let's think  

124
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:36,280
about the case where our compute situation is 
at this dotted line. If we're here, we should  

125
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:40,800
choose this last structure one as opposed to 
this even less structure one because the other  

126
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:45,760
one doesn't really work and the other one works.
But crucially, we need to remember that we are  

127
00:12:45,760 --> 00:12:50,840
adding some structure because we don't have 
compute, so we need to remove that later. And  

128
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:56,200
so the difference between these two methods is 
that additional inductive biases or structure we  

129
00:12:56,200 --> 00:13:03,800
impose, somewhat impose that typically don't get 
removed. So adding this. What that means is that,  

130
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:09,760
at the given level of compute data algorithmic 
development and architecture that we have,  

131
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:14,240
there's like an optimal inductive bias or 
structure that we can add to the problem  

132
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:19,400
to make the progress. And that has been 
really how we have made so much progress. 

133
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:23,840
But these are like shortcuts that hinder further 
scaling later on, so we have to remove them  

134
00:13:23,840 --> 00:13:29,280
later on when we have more compute, better 
algorithm, or whatever. And as a community,  

135
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:35,560
we do adding structure very well and because 
there's an incentive structure with like papers.  

136
00:13:35,560 --> 00:13:40,640
You add a nice one then you get a paper. But 
removing that doesn't really get you much,  

137
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:45,160
so that we don't really do that. And I 
think we should do a lot more of those. 

138
00:13:45,160 --> 00:13:51,560
So maybe another implication of this bitter lesson 
is that, because of this what is better in the  

139
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:59,200
long-term almost necessarily looks worse now. And 
this is quite unique to AI research because the  

140
00:13:59,200 --> 00:14:06,920
AI research of current paradigm is learning based 
method. Meaning that we are giving models freedom,  

141
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:12,400
the machines choose how they learn.
So because we need to give more freedom, it  

142
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:18,280
gives-- it's a more chaotic at the beginning, so 
it doesn't work. But then when it started working,  

143
00:14:18,280 --> 00:14:23,080
we can put in more compute and then it can be 
better, so it's really important to have this  

144
00:14:23,080 --> 00:14:30,560
in mind. So to summarize, we have identified this 
dominant driving force behind the AI research,  

145
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:36,280
and that is this exponentially cheaper 
compute, and associated scaling up. 

146
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:41,160
Now, that we have identified if you remember 
back from my initial slides, we-- the next  

147
00:14:41,160 --> 00:14:47,560
step is to understand this driving force better. 
And so that we're going to spend most of the time  

148
00:14:47,560 --> 00:14:53,160
doing that. And for that, we need to go back 
to some history of transformer because this is  

149
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:59,400
a transformers class and analyze key structures 
