00:17:56,200 --> 00:18:01,240
this a vector of size three or something. 
And then we have to let them take the dot  

183
00:18:01,240 --> 00:18:08,600
product. So this lines represent which element 
can talk to which element, other elements. 

184
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:13,360
And here because it's an input, we take what is 
called a bidirectional attention, so any token  

185
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:19,120
can talk to any other token. And then we have 
this MLP or feedforward layer which is per token,  

186
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:24,960
it doesn't have any interaction, we just do 
some multiplication just because we can do  

187
00:18:24,960 --> 00:18:31,840
it. And then that's one layer, and we repeat that 
n times, and that's just the transformer encoder. 

188
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:38,920
And at the end, what you get is the sequence of 
vectors each representing the sequence element  

189
00:18:38,920 --> 00:18:44,800
in this case a word. So that's the output of this 
encoder. Now, let's look at the decoder, which is  

190
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:54,000
similarly shaped stack of layers. So here we put 
in as an input what the answer should be. So here,  

191
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:57,840
BOS, is the beginning of sequence, and then das 
ist gut, I don't know how to pronounce it, but  

192
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:03,200
that's the German translation of, that is good.
And so we kind of go through the similar process.  

193
00:19:03,200 --> 00:19:08,240
Here we have a causal self-attention, 
meaning that the tokens of time step t can  

194
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:14,280
only attend to t and before because when we start 
generating it, we don't have the future tokens,  

195
00:19:14,280 --> 00:19:20,480
so we cannot-- when we train it, we should limit 
that, and that way, this is done by like masking,  

196
00:19:20,480 --> 00:19:27,520
but this is different from the encoder.
So after this, you can get-- after again,  

197
00:19:27,520 --> 00:19:34,200
n layers, you get this sequence output, and you 
have this-- the output of sequence, to sequence,  

198
00:19:34,200 --> 00:19:38,440
to sequence mapping, this is a general 
encoder-decoder architecture. And when you  

199
00:19:38,440 --> 00:19:43,640
get this end of sequence, you stop generating 
it. So this is the overall picture. Now,  

200
00:19:43,640 --> 00:19:51,160
I'll point out some important attention patterns.
So we are translating into German what is into  

201
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:54,840
input to the encoder. So there has to be 
some connection between the decoder and  

202
00:19:54,840 --> 00:19:59,040
the encoder that is done by this cross 
attention mechanism shown in this red,  

203
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:05,080
which is just that each vector representation of 
each sequence in the output decoder should attend  

204
00:20:05,080 --> 00:20:10,640
to some of them in the encoder, and that is done.
In particular the design feature which is  

205
00:20:10,640 --> 00:20:16,280
interesting is that all the layers in the 
decoder attend to the final layer output of the  

206
00:20:16,280 --> 00:20:23,680
encoder, and we'll come back to the implication 
of this design. So yeah, that's that. And now,  

207
00:20:23,680 --> 00:20:28,040
move on to the second type of architecture, which 
is encoder-only. We'll spend a little bit of time  

208
00:20:28,040 --> 00:20:35,680
here. So again, we have to-- we have the same 
input, and we go through a similar structure. 

209
00:20:35,680 --> 00:20:40,320
And then in this case, the final output is 
a single vector. Regardless of the length  

210
00:20:40,320 --> 00:20:47,120
of the sequence, we just get a single vector. 
And that is that represent the input sequence,  

211
00:20:47,120 --> 00:20:52,400
that's a dense vector representation. And then 
let's say we do some kind of a sentiment analysis,  

212
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:56,400
we run through a task specific linear 
layer to map it to classification  

213
00:20:56,400 --> 00:21:03,920
label positive or negative probabilities here.
And that's required for all these task specific  

214
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:09,320
cases. And this is kind of popularized 
by Bert. And what this means is that,  

215
00:21:09,320 --> 00:21:15,160
here at the time, 2018, when Bert came out 
we had the benchmark called glue, which was  

216
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:20,120
a language understanding task. You have a sequence 
in classification labels out for most cases. This  

217
00:21:20,120 --> 00:21:26,400
was how the field really advanced at the time.
So when we care about such tasks, then there's an  

218
00:21:26,400 --> 00:21:30,320
incentive to think about simplifying the problem, 
adding the structure to the problem so that we can  

219
00:21:30,320 --> 00:21:35,200
make a progress. So this, the additional structure 
that was put into this particular architecture is  

220
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:41,840
that we're going to give up on the generation. 
If we do that, it becomes a lot simpler problem.  

221
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:46,080
Instead of sequence to sequence, we're talking 
about sequence to classification labels,  

222
00:21:46,080 --> 00:21:51,720
and that's just so much easier.
And so at some point, 2018, 2019,  

223
00:21:51,720 --> 00:21:56,640
a lot of the papers are just research was like, 
we sometimes call it Bert engineers, a little  

224
00:21:56,640 --> 00:22:02,160
bit change of something get like 0.5% better on 
glue, and you get a paper, and things like that.  

225
00:22:02,160 --> 00:22:10,000
It was very chaotic error, and-- but if we look at 
from this perspective, we are putting the sequence  

226
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:16,120
structure of not generating the sequence that puts 
a lot of performance win, but in the long term,  

227
00:22:16,120 --> 00:22:21,320
it's not really useful. So we're not going to look 
at this encoder-only architecture going forward. 

228
00:22:21,320 --> 00:22:27,880
Third architecture, decoder-only. This one is 
my favorite personally. And it looks kind of  

229
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:34,240
daunting because of this attention pattern, 
but it actually is very simple. So here,  

230
00:22:34,240 --> 00:22:40,880
we only have a single stack, and it can actually 
generate stuff. And so there's misconception that  

231
00:22:40,880 --> 00:22:45,240
some people think this decoder-only architecture 
is used for language modeling next to prediction,  

232
00:22:45,240 --> 00:22:48,520
so it cannot be used for supervised 
learning. But here, we can actually do it. 

233
00:22:48,520 --> 00:22:54,040
The trick is to have this input that is good, 
concatenate it with the target. And if you do  

234
00:22:54,040 --> 00:23:00,080
that, then it just becomes simple to sequence in 
sequence out. So what we do is, the self-attention  

235
00:23:00,080 --> 00:23:05,760
mechanism here is actually handling both the 
cross attention between target and the input,  

236
00:23:05,760 --> 00:23:11,040
and self-attention sequence learning within 
each. So that's the causal attention. 

237
00:23:11,040 --> 00:23:16,720
And then as I mentioned, the output is a 
sequence, and then the key design features  

238
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:22,760
are self-attention is serving both roles, and 
we are in some sense sharing the parameters  

239
00:23:22,760 --> 00:23:26,840
between input and target. So same set of 
parameters are applied to both input and  

240
00:23:26,840 --> 00:23:32,520
the target sequences. So this is the decoder 
only, now, we will go into the comparison. 

241
00:23:32,520 --> 00:23:37,760
So I think there are many-- like they look 
very different, at least, on the schematics.  

242
00:23:37,760 --> 00:23:44,960
So how different are they actually? And I argue 
that they're actually quite similar. And so to  

243
00:23:44,960 --> 00:23:48,760
illustrate that we're going to transform 
starting from this encoder-decoder which  

244
00:23:48,760 --> 00:23:53,880
has more structures built in, and then 
into the decoder-only architecture and  

245
00:23:53,880 --> 00:23:58,680
see what are some of the differences. And then 
interpret those differences, those additional  

246
00:23:58,680 --> 00:24:04,560
structures, are they relevant nowadays now that 
we have more compute better algorithm and so on? 

247
00:24:04,560 --> 00:24:10,200
So let's have this table, four differences we'll 
see each of them. And then as we go through,  

248
00:24:10,200 --> 00:24:16,080
we will populate this table. So let's first 
look at this additional cross-attention. What  

249
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:20,400
that means is that, this on the left is an 
encoder-decoder, which has this additional  

250
00:24:20,400 --> 00:24:24,320
red block, the cross-attention compared 
to the simpler one that doesn't have that. 

251
00:24:24,320 --> 00:24:30,640
So we want to make it, make the left closer to the 
right, so that means we need to either get rid of  

252
00:24:30,640 --> 00:24:36,000
it or something. And attention mechanism 
has kind of the four projection matrices,  

253
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,640
and so self-attention and cross attention 
actually have the same number of parameters,  

254
00:24:39,640 --> 00:24:43,600
same shape, so we can just share them. So 
that's the first step, share both of these,  

255
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:48,880
and then it becomes mostly the same mechanism.
And then so that's the first difference separate  

256
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:54,320
cross-attention or self-attention serving both 
roles. Second difference is the parameter sharing.  

257
00:24:54,320 --> 00:25:00,280
So what that means is that, between the input and 
the target, encoder-decoder architecture uses a  

258
00:25:00,280 --> 00:25:05,800
separate parameters. And decoder-only only has 
a single stack so it uses the shared parameter. 

259
00:25:05,800 --> 00:25:11,280
So if we want to make the left close to right, we 
want to share the encoder parameters, so let's do  

260
00:25:11,280 --> 00:25:16,600
that. I just color this, so now, they share the 
parameters. Third difference is the target to  

261
00:25:16,600 --> 00:25:21,800
input attention pattern. So we need to connect the 
target to the input. And how does that-- how is it  

262
00:25:21,800 --> 00:25:27,400
done? In the encoder-decoder case, we had this 
cross attention. And then in the decoder-only,  

263
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:35,520
it's a per the self-attention doing everything.
What the difference is that we have this every  

264
00:25:35,520 --> 00:25:40,800
layer of the decoder attending to the final layer 
output of the encoder, whereas if you think about  

265
00:25:40,800 --> 00:25:47,760
this decoder is actually per layer, within 
layer when we are decoding the say word thus,  

266
00:25:47,760 --> 00:25:53,560
we are looking at the same layer representation 
of the encoder, and that's within layer,  

267
00:25:53,560 --> 00:25:57,560
and I think this is the design feature. So if 
we want to make this close to that, we have  

268
00:25:57,560 --> 00:26:03,480
to bring back this attention to each layer.
So now, layer 1 will be attending to layer  

269
00:26:03,480 --> 00:26:09,640
1 of this. And that's then finally, the 
last difference is the input attention.  

270
00:26:09,640 --> 00:26:15,160
I mentioned about this bidirectional attention. 
And because we have this decoder only typically,  

271
00:26:15,160 --> 00:26:21,040
with the unidirectional attention, we need to make 
them matching, so that's the we can just get rid  

272
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:27,520
of it. I just got rid of the some of the arrows.
So then at this point, these two architectures  

273
00:26:27,520 --> 00:26:31,400
are almost identical. A little bit 
of difference in the cross attention,  

274
00:26:31,400 --> 00:26:36,600
but same number of parameters. And if you have 
in deep learning, if you just train these, these  

275
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:40,640
two architecture in the same task, same data, I 
think, you will get pretty much within the noise,  

276
00:26:40,640 --> 00:26:46,240
probably closer than if you train the same 
thing twice. So I would say they are identical. 

277
00:26:46,240 --> 00:26:51,800
And so these are the main differences. Now, 
we'll look at what are the additional structures,  

278
00:26:51,800 --> 00:26:57,520
what they mean, it means. So yeah. 
That's the populated table now,  

279
00:26:57,520 --> 00:27:03,120
and then so we can say that encoder-decoder 
compared to the decoder-only architecture has  

280
00:27:03,120 --> 00:27:09,360
these additional structures inductive biases 
built in, so let's go into each of them. 

281
00:27:09,360 --> 00:27:15,800
The first one is what encoder-decoder tries added 
as a structure is that, input and the target  

282
00:27:15,800 --> 00:27:21,160
sequences are sufficiently different that we-- 
it will be useful to use a separate parameters,  

283
00:27:21,160 --> 00:27:27,600
that's the assumption. And so why is that a 
useful? When can that assumption be useful?  

284
00:27:27,600 --> 00:27:32,960
And one example is machine translation. Back 
when the transformer was introduced in 2017,  

285
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:37,240
translation was really popular task, and 
it was difficult, considered difficult. 

286
00:27:37,240 --> 00:27:42,160
And because it's a sequence-to-sequence and 
you can actually have a blue score which is  

287
00:27:42,160 --> 00:27:48,080
heuristic based method that can give you a single 
number and then people can optimize that. So what  

288
00:27:48,080 --> 00:27:52,680
that in that task, we have this input and 
target in completely different languages. So  

289
00:27:52,680 --> 00:27:57,400
if the goal is to learn translation only, 
then it kind of makes sense to have, OK,  

290
00:27:57,400 --> 00:28:01,880
this parameter in the encoder will take care of 
the English, and this parameter in the decoder  

291
00:28:01,880 --> 00:28:08,400
will take care of the German, that seems natural.
And what about now? Modern language models is  

292
00:28:08,400 --> 00:28:13,040
about learning knowledge. And it's not just 
about translation or not even about language,  

293
00:28:13,040 --> 00:28:18,680
languages comes up as a byproduct of doing 
this next token prediction and translation as  

294
00:28:18,680 --> 00:28:25,440
well. So does it make sense to have a separate 
parameter for this kind of situation now? We  

295
00:28:25,440 --> 00:28:31,200
have some knowledge in German, some knowledge in 
English, and we-- if anything we want to combine  

296
00:28:31,200 --> 00:28:35,800
them. And if we represent them in a separate 
parameters, I don't think that's natural. 

297
00:28:35,800 --> 00:28:42,600
So I would say with this much more general, 
larger models that can do a lot of things,  

298
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:48,720
this assumption seems very unnatural to me. Second 
example is a little bit more modern. Two years ago  

299
00:28:48,720 --> 00:28:55,840
when I was at Google and with Jason, we did this 
instruction fine tuning work. And what this is,  

300
00:28:55,840 --> 00:29:01,520
is you take the pre-trained model, and then just 
fine tune on academic data set, and so that it  

301
00:29:01,520 --> 00:29:06,440
can understand the natural language instruction.
So the detail doesn't matter, but here let's think  

302
00:29:06,440 --> 00:29:13,160
about the performance gain by doing this fine 
tuning on two different architectures we tried. So  

303
00:29:13,160 --> 00:29:19,080
first five is the Flan-T5, which is T5 base which 
is the encoder-decoder architecture. Last one,  

304
00:29:19,080 --> 00:29:27,040
the latter five decoder-only architecture based 
on PaLM. So we spent 99% of the time on PaLM,  

305
00:29:27,040 --> 00:29:31,600
optimizing a lot of these, and then at the 
end we just spent like three days on T5. 

306
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:36,640
But the performance gain was a lot higher on 
this. And I was really confused about this,  

307
00:29:36,640 --> 00:29:41,560
and in a very good way. And after the paper 
was published, I wanted to take a little bit  

308
00:29:41,560 --> 00:29:49,080
deeper into why this might be the case. So my 
hypothesis is that, it's about the length. So  

309
00:29:49,080 --> 00:29:56,040
academic data sets we use, we use like 1,832 
tasks. And here they have this very distinctive  

310
00:29:56,040 --> 00:30:01,040
characteristic where we have a long input, 
long in order to make the task more difficult. 

311
00:30:01,040 --> 00:30:06,000
But then we cannot make the target long 
because if we do, there's no way to create it,  

312
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:10,600
so there's fundamental challenge of that. So 
what happens is, you have a long text of input  

313
00:30:10,600 --> 00:30:16,120
and then short text of the target. And so this 
is kind of the length distribution of what it  

314
00:30:16,120 --> 00:30:23,520
went into the Flan fine tuning. So then you 
see this, you have a very different sequence  

315
00:30:23,520 --> 00:30:28,120
going into the encoder as an input, and the very 
different type of sequence going into the target. 

316
00:30:28,120 --> 00:30:33,400
So now, this encoder-decoder architecture has 
an assumption that they will be very different.  

317
00:30:33,400 --> 00:30:39,560
That structure really shines because of this. 
It was a kind of an accident, but that was,  

318
00:30:39,560 --> 00:30:45,760
I think, why this really architecture was just 
suitable for fine tuning with the academic data  

319
00:30:45,760 --> 00:30:52,160
sets. What about now? Do we care about this 
kind of assumption? And if you think about the  

320
00:30:52,160 --> 00:30:58,000
general use cases of language models nowadays, 
if anything the more interesting cases involve  

321
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:03,240
longer generation, longer target.
Just because we cannot grade them,  

322
00:31:03,240 --> 00:31:07,000
doesn't mean that we are not interested in them. 
Actually, if anything we are more interested  

323
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:12,720
in that. So now we have this longer target 
situation, so this separate sequence length  

324
00:31:12,720 --> 00:31:18,680
parameter doesn't seem to make much sense. And 
moreover, we think about this chat application  

325
00:31:18,680 --> 00:31:24,440
like ChatGPT, we do multi-turn conversation.
And then so what is a target of this turn  

326
00:31:24,440 --> 00:31:29,520
becomes the input of the next turn. And then 
my question is, does that make sense to even  

327
00:31:29,520 --> 00:31:37,480
think about a different parameters if next turn 
is going to be the same thing? So that was the  

328
00:31:37,480 --> 00:31:43,240
first inductive bias we just mentioned. And then 
the second structure is that, target element can  

329
00:31:43,240 --> 00:31:48,640
only attend to the fully encoded ones, the 
final output of the encoder. Let's look at  

330
00:31:48,640 --> 00:31:54,400
this additional structure, what that means.
So as I mentioned, we have this very top  

331
00:31:54,400 --> 00:32:00,880
layer attending to it. And so in deep neural nets, 
typically, we see that the bottom layers and the  

332
00:32:00,880 --> 00:32:06,360
top layers encode information at a very different 
level. Meaning that, for example, in computer  

333
00:32:06,360 --> 00:32:11,920
vision, lower layer-- the bottom layers encode 
something like edges, top layers, higher levels  

334
00:32:11,920 --> 00:32:17,480
combining the features, something like cat face.
And so we call this deep learning a hierarchical  

335
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:25,120
representation learning method. And so now the 
question is, if decoder layer one attends to  

336
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:29,360
encoder final layer, which probably has 
a very different level of information,  

337
00:32:29,360 --> 00:32:34,960
is that some kind of an information bottleneck 
which actually motivated the original attention  

338
00:32:34,960 --> 00:32:40,560
mechanism? And in practice I would say in my 
experience doesn't really make any difference,  

339
00:32:40,560 --> 00:32:46,400
and that's because my experience was limited 
to say 25, 24 layers of encoder of T5. 

340
00:32:46,400 --> 00:32:52,200
So layer one attended to 24, probably fine. But 
what if we have 10x or 1,000x more layers, would  

341
00:32:52,200 --> 00:32:57,840
that be problematic? I'm not really comfortable 
with that. So I think this is also unnecessary  

342
00:32:59,280 --> 00:33:05,520
design that maybe we need to revisit. Final 
structure we're going to talk about is the,  

343
00:33:05,520 --> 00:33:10,640
when we do this, there's a bi-directional thing 
in the encoder-decoder. Let's think about that. 

344
00:33:10,640 --> 00:33:18,400
So yeah. Bi-directional input attention, is 
that really necessary? So when we had this Bert,  

345
00:33:18,400 --> 00:33:24,400
B in Bert stands for bi-directional. 2018 when 
we were solving like question answering squad,  

346
00:33:24,400 --> 00:33:29,160
actually it was very difficult task. So if you 
have any additional trick, it can make a huge  

347
00:33:29,160 --> 00:33:35,200
difference. Bidirectionality was really useful, 
I think, maybe boosting up the squad score by  

348
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:40,480
like 20, so it was really huge thing. But at 
scale, I don't think this matters that much. 

349
00:33:40,480 --> 00:33:47,240
This is my highly anecdotal experience, so we 
did in Flan-2, we tried both bi-directional and  

350
00:33:47,240 --> 00:33:53,360
uni-directional fine tuning, didn't really make 
much difference. So but I want to point out this  

351
00:33:53,360 --> 00:33:59,560
bidirectionality, actually bring in an engineering 
challenge for modern multi-turn chat application.  

352
00:33:59,560 --> 00:34:05,280
So at every turn, the new input has to be encoded.
Again, and for uni-directional attention it's  

353
00:34:05,280 --> 00:34:10,160
much, much better, so here is what I mean by 
that. So let's think about this more modern  

354
00:34:10,160 --> 00:34:15,440
conversation between user and assistant, 
how are you? Bad, and why? And so here,  

355
00:34:15,440 --> 00:34:21,280
if we think about the bi-directional case, we 
will-- and when we generate bad, we need to encode  

356
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:27,360
this input with the bi-directional thing, which 
is fine. And then after the bad is generated,  

357
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:34,000
when we're trying to generate why, we'll need to 
encode how, again, because how can attend to bad,  

358
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:39,720
so we need to do everything from scratch again.
In contrast, if we do uni-directional one,  

359
00:34:39,720 --> 00:34:45,800
we can do much, much better because now when we 
are trying to generate why, we don't have to redo  

360
00:34:45,800 --> 00:34:52,160
how because we cannot attend to the future tokens, 
so we don't have to do anything. So if you see the  

361
00:34:52,160 --> 00:34:58,000
difference, this part can be cached and then this 
part is the only thing that can has to be encoded  

362
00:34:58,000 --> 00:35:03,600
again. So this kind of makes a big difference 
when we think about multiple turns going on. 

363
00:35:03,600 --> 00:35:09,720
So I would say, bi-directional attention, that 
well, in 2018, which is mostly sold by scale,  

